---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';

/* WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS, and why it was missing for so long.
   The site collects a name, an email, a company and four narrow answers through
   ContactForm.jsx and POSTs them to /api/contact, and src/scripts/track.js posts
   click events to /api/event. California's online privacy law
   (Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code §§ 22575-22579) requires any commercial site
   collecting personal details from its residents to post a policy, and it
   reaches operators OUTSIDE California — no revenue threshold, no size
   exemption. There was no privacy route in src/pages/ at all until 2026-08-27.

   It also unblocks things that have nothing to do with privacy law: business
   text-message registration and several ad platforms refuse an applicant whose
   site has no reachable policy.

   THE STATUTE WAS READ RATHER THAN RECALLED, 2026-09-03, and the page was
   checked against subsection (b) clause by clause. Four of its six were already
   here: the categories collected and who they go to, how to ask for a change,
   how a change to this policy is announced, and a date. TWO WERE MISSING and
   are the "Do not track" and "Anyone else watching" sections below. The claim
   is registered in src/data/external-claims.js so it ages rather than being
   trusted forever.

   THE CCPA LAYER IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT AND THAT IS A DECISION, not an
   oversight. Its thresholds are roughly $26.6M revenue, 100,000 California
   residents' data a year, or half of revenue from selling data — none is close.
   A "Do Not Sell or Share" link here would claim to be governed by a law that
   does not reach this business, which is a false statement in the one document
   whose entire value is being accurate.

   EVERY CLAIM BELOW IS TRUE OF WHAT THIS SITE ACTUALLY DOES. Do not copy a
   generic policy over it. If the contact form, the logging or the tracking ever
   changes, this page changes with it IN THE SAME COMMIT — and
   `npm run gate` now refuses a build where an outside service the site actually
   reaches is not named on this page.

   TWO CLAIMS HERE WERE TOO NARROW UNTIL 2026-09-03 and both are corrected
   below. The form section said a message "is emailed to me" while the Worker
   also writes it to Cloudflare's log first, and the counting section described
   Cloudflare as keeping "ordinary server records" while a counting script runs
   in the visitor's own browser. Neither was a lie and both were less than the
   whole thing, which on this page is the same failure. */

/* The statute asks for an EFFECTIVE date, not a "last updated" one. Same value,
   and the word it uses is the word it wants. */
const EFFECTIVE = '3 September 2026';
---
<MarketingLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site collects, why, and what happens to it. Short, and true of what the site actually does."
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-line">
    <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-24">
      <p class="mono-label">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl font-display text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-5xl">
        What this site collects.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Short, because there is not much of it. In effect from {EFFECTIVE}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14 sm:py-20">
    <div class="space-y-10 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">The short version</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The only thing this site collects about you is what you type into the contact form and
          press send. <strong class="text-ink">The tools do not collect anything at all</strong> —
          they run entirely in your browser, and nothing you type into one is sent anywhere, stored
          by anyone, or seen by me. That is not a policy I could quietly change later; it is how
          they are built.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">When you use the contact form</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          It asks for your name, your email address, optionally your business, and your answers to
          four short questions about the work. That is emailed to me. I read every one myself —
          there is no sales team and no customer-relationship system it lands in.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <strong class="text-ink">One thing worth being exact about.</strong> A moment before that
          email is sent, what you wrote is also written to this site's own server log at Cloudflare.
          That exists so a message is not simply lost if the email fails to send, which is the only
          way I would ever know you had tried. Cloudflare keeps those logs for a limited period and
          then discards them. It is a safety net rather than a record I go looking through.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          I keep those emails for as long as they are useful, and I do not add you to any mailing
          list. There is no newsletter here to be signed up to.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The form is delivered by <strong class="text-ink">Resend</strong>, an email service, and
          the site runs on <strong class="text-ink">Cloudflare</strong>. Both handle your message
          only in order to deliver it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">When you use one of the tools</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Nothing leaves the page. No account, no login, no upload. Even a spreadsheet you drop into
          one is read by the page itself and never sent. Every tool on this site says so on its own
          panel, and it is true of all of them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">When we meet on a video call</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          A call runs through Daily, a video service, and the picture and sound pass through their
          servers to reach us both. That is how any browser video call works — two computers on
          ordinary networks cannot usually reach each other directly.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <strong class="text-ink">I record only if I ask you first.</strong> If a call is being
          recorded, your screen shows an indicator the whole time, so you never have to take my word
          for it. A recording is stored by that same video service, and I delete it when it has done
          its job. Say no and we simply talk — nothing about the call depends on it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">If you pay me</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Payments are handled by <strong class="text-ink">Stripe</strong>, whether I send you an
          invoice or you pay for something here directly. They get your name, your email address and
          the amount, because that is what an invoice needs.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <strong class="text-ink">Your card details go to Stripe and never to this site or to
          me.</strong> Paying happens on a page Stripe runs, not on a page I built, so the numbers
          never pass through anything of mine. That is not a policy I could quietly change later —
          it is a term in every agreement I sign with a client.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">What is counted</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          I count how often certain links get clicked — which pages people open, which tools get
          tried. <strong class="text-ink">That count records what was clicked, not who clicked
          it.</strong> Alongside it the site records which page you arrived from and which country
          you are in, which arrives with the request rather than being asked for.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <strong class="text-ink">There is also a small counting script from Cloudflare on every
          page.</strong> It records how many times a page was opened, which site a visitor came from,
          and how quickly the page drew. I use it to tell whether my own pages are fast. It sets no
          cookie, it does not identify you, and it cannot follow you anywhere else.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          There is no advertising tracker on this site, no product building a profile of you, and
          nothing here is sold or shared with anyone for advertising.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Anyone else watching</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <strong class="text-ink">No.</strong> No other company can watch what you do here, or
          follow you from this site to other sites over time. The only outside services this site
          touches are the ones named on this page, each doing one job, and none of them is in the
          advertising business.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">"Do not track"</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Some browsers can send a "do not track" request to every site you visit.
          <strong class="text-ink">This site does not respond to it, and the honest reason is that
          there is nothing for it to switch off.</strong> The thing that signal exists to stop —
          being followed from site to site and profiled — is not something this site does to anyone,
          with or without the request.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Cookies</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          This site sets none of its own. It does keep one thing in your browser while the tab is
          open: a random number with nothing attached to it, so that a handful of clicks in one
          visit can be counted as one visit rather than several. <strong class="text-ink">It is
          thrown away the moment you close the tab</strong>, it is not a cookie, and it says nothing
          about who you are.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">What I will never do</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Sell your information. Share it for advertising. Pass it to anyone except the services
          named above that are needed to deliver your message to me.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Asking me to delete it</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Email me and ask what I hold about you, ask me to correct it, or ask me to delete it. I
          will do it and reply within 30 days. You do not have to give a reason.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Children</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          This site is not aimed at children under 13, and I do not knowingly collect anything from
          them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Security</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          I take reasonable care with what I hold. <strong class="text-ink">No website can promise
          perfect security</strong> and I am not going to pretend otherwise. If something ever went
          wrong involving personal information, I would tell the people affected and the North
          Carolina Attorney General, as state law requires.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">If this changes</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The date at the top changes with it, and the new version replaces this one here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel panel--flat rounded-lg p-6">
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Who I am</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Ian David Provencher, doing business as AppliedIQ Solutions — a sole proprietorship in
          New Bern, North Carolina.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <a class="link-quiet" href="/contact/">Contact me</a> · every message comes straight to me.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
